! Silicon, sixty-four atoms: the cubic cell of si8-1k.in stacked eight deep.
!
! Twice si32-1k.in -- 256 electrons, 128 bands -- in a tetragonal cell with
! c/a = 8, built by the same rule that made si32 from si16 and si16 from si8.
! The largest cell in this directory, and it exists to be the one case where a
! GPU is asked what it is actually worth.
!
! **The energy per atom is not comparable across this ladder** and that is
! sampling, not a defect: doubling along z halves the zone, so one k-point of
! this cell folds two of si32's. The cell is verified two ways that do not
! depend on sampling -- every atom is 4-coordinated at exactly a*sqrt(3)/4, and
! si64 at Gamma reproduces si32 sampled at {Gamma, (0,0,1/2)} to 2.9e-09
! Ry/atom -- the folding identity, at this cell's own SCF convergence floor
! rather than at machine precision, because 1e-10 on a 504 Ry total is 2e-13
! relative and 64 atoms divides it. si32's version of the same check is exact
! to 8.9e-16. See si32-1k.in.
!
! Single k-point, like every other file here: both codes parallelise over k, so
! a multi-k comparison measures batching rather than the cost of the physics.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav=6, celldm(1) =10.20, celldm(3) =8.0,
    nat=64, ntyp=1,
    ecutwfc=12.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS crystal
